#### Normalized Distance Distribution of Photons

The distribution of photons relative to the expected Cherenkov Fiducial area as shown in Figure 4.30 shows strong indications of a peak in the position expected for the \(\pi\) photons, with a somewhat weaker response in the K channel near \(d_{\pi}\) values \(\sim-0.5\). This apparently poor resolution in the separation of the \(\pi\) and K (as well as P) photon peaks seen in the normalized \(d_{\pi}\) distribution can be improved by cutting away the forward section of the Cherenkov Fiducial area as discussed in the previous section.

The Cherenkov Fiducial area is narrow and well defined in the back portion of the ring, near \(|\psi\prime|\sim 180^{\circ}\) for track's striking the RICH radiator at any angle, whereas the forward section (\(|\psi\prime|\sim 0^{\circ}\)) of the Cherenkov Fiducial area deviates strongly from the circular shape for track's having angles of inclination \(\theta_{inc}\gtrsim 10^{\circ}\). The Cherenkov Fiducial area in the forward region is very large and is characterized by local ring widths which are much larger than what is calculated in the back portion. This rapid variation in the local ring width for tracks inclined relative to the RICH radiator results in a loss of resolution in the \(d_{\pi}\) distribution.

To overcome this, a cut can be applied to remove photons found in the forward portion of the rings. The constant area angle (see Figure 4.24 and eq. 4.21) can be used to remove photons having reconstructed azimuthal angles \(\gamma_{reco.photon}<\psi\prime_{cut-off}\). Figures 4.33 and 4.34 illustrate the effectiveness of the cut. The weak K peak in the \(d_{\pi}\) distribution, Figure4.33, is seen with much better clarity after applying the constant area angle cut, as seen in Figure 4.34.

The normalized distribution of photons found within a predicted Cherenkov Fiducial area is approximately Gaussian. Figure 4.35 shows the normalized distance distribution of photons relative to the predicted \(\pi\) Cherenkov Fiducial area for \(\sim 2000\) tracks within a limited range in transverse momenta, \(1.49<p_{\perp}[GeV/c]<1.7\). The tracks contained in this sample are well above the K and P momentum thresholds, (\(\sim.6\) and \(\sim 1.2~{}GeV/c\)) and thus it is expected (and seen) that photon peaks for the K and P should be present in the \(d_{\pi}\) distribution.

The values plotted for \(d_{\pi}\) range from a lower limit of -4 to an upper limit of 10. The lower limit corresponds to points well inside the \(\pi\) radius while the upper limit represents photons far removed from the outer radius of the Cherenkov Fiducial area predicted for the \(\pi\). In addition to the three photon peaks present in the Figure, a smooth background is present extending over the entire range of \(d_{\pi}\) values. The background is relatively small for small values of \(d_{\pi}\) values and grows large as \(d_{\pi}\) increases in value. Large values of \(d_{\pi}\) correspond to large radii, and thus greater amounts of bac

Figure 4.35: Example of normalized distance distribution of photons relative to the \(\pi\) Cherenkov Fiducial area for \(1.49<p_{\perp}GeV/c<1.7\). Photons falling within the \(\pi\) fiducial area are highlighted in red, while photons corresponding to the K fiducial area are shown in blue, with the photons for the P in green. A cut to remove photons in the forward region of the predicted Cherenkov Fiducial area has been applied.

large values of \(d_{\pi}\).

The \(\pi\), K and P photon peaks in Figure 4.35 have been fitted to Gaussian curves simultaneously with a third order polynomial for the background. The Gaussian fit to the \(\pi\) photon peak is highlighted in red, along with the K (blue) and P (green) fits. The result of the polynomial fit to the background is shown in the same figure (magenta). The mean, \(\overline{d_{\pi}}\), and width, \(\sigma_{\pi}\), of the Gaussian fit to the \(\pi\) photon peak is plotted vs the track's transverse momentum in Figure 4.36. The fits were performed for positive and negative tracks separately, with the blue data points representing negative tracks and red data points for the positive tracks. The Gaussian means and widths extracted from the \(d_{\pi}\) fits show similar behavior for the positive and negative tracks and are flat as a function of the track's primary \(p_{\perp}\). The photons found within the \(\pi\) Cherenkov Fiducial area have a Gaussian mean \(\sim 0.54\) and a width \(\sim 0.27\). Similar trends are found for the means and widths when plotted against the track's angle of incidence or along the ring's \(\psi\prime\) parameter.

